---
import pkg from '../../package.json';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { buildOutline, href, lessonPath, PLAYGROUNDS, playgroundPath } from '../site';
import { lessonEntries } from '../lib/lessons';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const outline = buildOutline(await lessonEntries());
const first = outline[0].lessons[0];
const lessons = outline.flatMap((part) => part.lessons);
/** Link to a lesson by its number, so the home page follows any renaming. */
const lessonHref = (order: number) => href(base, lessonPath(lessons.find((l) => l.order === order)!.id));
---

<Base title="Singer Difference Sets" description="An interactive introduction to Singer difference sets, finite fields, and projective planes.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <h1>Singer Difference Sets</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Mark 0, 1, and 3 on a clock with seven hours. Every distance from 1 to 6 hours appears exactly once between
        two of the marks. Sets of numbers like this exist at arbitrarily large sizes: 4 marks on 13 hours, 6 on 31,
        33 on 1057, and on without end. Finding one might seem to need an expensive brute-force search through every
        possible set of marks, but in 1938 James Singer showed how to write one down directly for every prime power,
        using finite fields and projective planes.
      </p>
      <p>
        These lessons explain how his method works and why, starting from clock arithmetic and assuming only high-school
        algebra. Along the way the same sets turn out to deal a <a href={lessonHref(20)}>card game</a>, take{' '}
        <a href={lessonHref(21)}>pictures without a lens</a>, <a href={lessonHref(22)}>find a radar echo</a>,{' '}
        <a href={lessonHref(23)}>correct errors</a> in broadcast data, and schedule a <a href={lessonHref(26)}>fair race</a>.
      </p>
      <p><a class="button" href={href(base, lessonPath(first.id))}>Start with lesson 1: {first.title}</a></p>
      <p class="print-link">
        Prefer paper? <a href={href(base, 'singer-difference-sets.pdf')}>Download the printable PDF</a> (v{pkg.version}):
        every lesson, with the figures as snapshots, a glossary, and an index.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div data-widget="chord-diagram" data-set="0,1,3"></div>
  </section>

  <h2>How to read this</h2>
  <ul class="how-to-read">
    <li>
      <strong>Lessons 1 to 16 are the main path.</strong> Lessons 1 to 5 turn the puzzle into geometry, 6 to 10 build the
      finite fields, 11 to 14 build a plane from a field and prove Singer's theorem, and{' '}
      <a href={lessonHref(15)}>Lesson 15</a> gives the algorithm, with the whole argument on one page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every lesson has an interactive figure.</strong> Use it before reading the argument under it. Each lesson
      ends with a <em>Try it</em> exercise, with its answer folded away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The folded "Why…" boxes hold the longer proofs.</strong> The main thread does not depend on them, so they
      can wait for a second reading.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Words with a dotted underline are in the <a href={href(base, 'glossary/')}>glossary</a>.</strong> Hover
      or tap for the definition.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The applications can be read early.</strong> Most of{' '}
      <a href={lessonHref(20)}>Part VII</a> depends only on Lessons 1 to 5.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The path</h2>
  {
    outline.map((part) => (
      <section class="path-part">
        <h3>
          Part {part.numeral}: {part.title}
        </h3>
        <ol class="path-list">
          {part.lessons.map((l) => (
            <li value={l.order}>
              <a href={href(base, lessonPath(l.id))}>{l.title}</a>
              <span class="summary">{l.summary}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Playgrounds</h2>
  <ul class="path-list">
    {
      PLAYGROUNDS.map((pg) => (
        <li>
          <a href={href(base, playgroundPath(pg.slug))}>{pg.title}</a>
          <span class="summary">{pg.summary}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</Base>
